    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what do you mean by that goblin analogy? I'm not sure I follow. Can we just stick to the science? Specifically, how exactly does the rye protein interact with the lactic acid bacteria versus the yeast to cause that sudden spike in acidity?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sorry about the detour into goblin lore. I was just trying to illustrate the unpredictable nature of fermentation, but I suppose it's not entirely relevant to the science.\n\nNow, let's get back to the nitty-gritty. The rye protein in your mix can interact with the lactic acid bacteria (LAB) and yeast in a few ways that might contribute to the sudden spike in acidity.\n\nOne possibility is that the high protein content in rye flour can lead to the production of peptides and other nitrogen-containing compounds. These compounds can be fermented by the LAB, which can produce more lactic acid as a byproduct. This can cause a rapid increase in acidity, especially if the LAB are not well-established in the levain.\n\nAnother possibility is that the yeast in your levain are competing with the LAB for nutrients, such as nitrogen and carbon sources. If the yeast are able to outcompete the LAB, they may produce more carbon dioxide and ethanol, which can lead to a buildup of acidic compounds.\n\nIt's also worth noting that the type of rye flour you're using can affect the protein content and the resulting fermentation behavior. Some ryes, like dark or whole rye, may have a higher protein content than others",
